# libFuzzer's *working* corpus. It reads from every directory on the command line and writes
# every newly-discovered input back into the **first** one, so the two are kept apart: `seeds/`
# is committed and only ever read, `corpus/` is where the machine writes.
#
# The per-target directories themselves are tracked (via `.gitkeep`) rather than left to be
# created, because libFuzzer errors out on a corpus directory that does not exist — so a fresh
# clone would fail the very command README.md documents.
/corpus/*/*
!/corpus/*/.gitkeep

# Crash/timeout/OOM reproducers. One worth keeping becomes a named regression test in the crate
# that owns the bug — as F-01, F-02 and the `parse_number` infinity did — not a committed blob.
/artifacts/
/coverage/
/target/

# Not committed, deliberately. No CI job resolves this crate, so a committed lockfile here
# would only rot unnoticed, and a fuzz campaign's value does not depend on a pinned dependency
# set. The host workspace's `Cargo.lock` is committed and gate-checked; this is not that.
Cargo.lock
